IMPORT `er1-seat`

-- Worked cases for the seat layer er1-seat (Employment Insurance Regulations, regs 1 to 18). Piece er1.
-- Every expected value is written by hand from the text of the Regulations (../../registers/source-bundle/SOR-96-332.txt), beside the assertion, before the
-- question was run: the person's facts are the facts a real person in the seat would state, and the answer is what the cited
-- regulation says of them. Each question has at least one case; most have a case that goes the other way. The people and the figures
-- are invented. Calendar: Sunday 18 October 2026, Saturday 24 October 2026, Sunday 25 October 2026.

§ `Regulation 1 -- family member, guardian, ward, care, support, critically ill, nurse practitioner, international organization`

-- Amara looks after her grandmother (paragraph (e)), Ben after a neighbour, Chidi after a friend he thinks of as a brother (paragraph (q)).
-- arguments: (a) spouse; (b) child; (c) parent; (d) sibling; (e) grandparent; (f) grandchild; (g) child's spouse; (h) spouse's parent; (i) sibling's spouse; (j) spouse's sibling; (k) uncle or aunt; (l) nephew or niece; (m) foster parent; (n) foster child; (o) ward; (p) guardian; (q) like a close relative
#ASSERT `Is the person I care for or support my family member under regulation 1(3)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the person I care for or support my family member under regulation 1(3)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is the person I care for or support my family member under regulation 1(3)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
-- Dilnaz's brother-in-law (her husband's sibling, paragraph (j)) and a woman who was her foster child's wife (paragraph (n)).
#ASSERT `Is the person I care for or support my family member under regulation 1(3)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is the person I care for or support my family member under regulation 1(3)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Esra asks whether her uncle's curator counts as a guardian; Felipe whether a woman with no legal authority does.
-- arguments: authorized for a minor; authorized for an incapable adult; a tutor; a curator; a mandatary under a protection mandate; appointed in a similar capacity
#ASSERT `Is this person a guardian under regulation 1(4)` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is this person a guardian under regulation 1(4)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is this person a guardian under regulation 1(4)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is this person my ward under regulation 1(4)` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is this person my ward under regulation 1(4)` FALSE)

-- Gina feeds, washes and moves her sick father (care); Hamid, a nurse, gives the same help as part of his job (a health care professional).
-- arguments: the person helped is a family member / critically ill child / adult; required because of the state of health; provided by a health care professional
#ASSERT `Does the help I give count as care under regulation 1(5)` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the help I give count as care under regulation 1(5)` TRUE TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the help I give count as care under regulation 1(5)` TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- Ines keeps her sister company through chemotherapy (support has no exception for professionals).
#ASSERT `Does the help I give count as support under regulation 1(5)` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the help I give count as support under regulation 1(5)` TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the help I give count as support under regulation 1(5)` FALSE TRUE)

-- Jonas's son was born on 2 May 2016; the period begins on 5 October 2026 (he is 10). Kaveh's son turned 18 on 4 October 2026, the day before.
-- arguments: date of birth; the day the period begins; baseline state of health has significantly changed; life is at risk
#ASSERT `Is the child I am caring for a critically ill child under regulation 1(6)` (YMD 2016 5 2) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the child I am caring for a critically ill child under regulation 1(6)` (YMD 2008 10 4) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the child I am caring for a critically ill child under regulation 1(6)` (YMD 2016 5 2) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is the adult I am caring for a critically ill adult under regulation 1(7)` (YMD 1958 1 1) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `Is the adult I am caring for a critically ill adult under regulation 1(7)` (YMD 2008 10 4) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the adult I am caring for a critically ill adult under regulation 1(7)` (YMD 2016 5 2) (YMD 2026 10 5) TRUE TRUE)

-- Lena's doctor is a registered nurse allowed to practise as a nurse practitioner and to diagnose, order tests, prescribe and treat on her own; Mateo's nurse cannot prescribe.
-- arguments: registered nurse; entitled to practise as a nurse practitioner; diagnoses; orders and interprets tests; prescribes; treats
#ASSERT `Is my health practitioner a nurse practitioner under regulation 1(3)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my health practitioner a nurse practitioner under regulation 1(3)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)

-- Nadia works for the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (a specialized agency Canada belongs to, tied to the UN by article 63); Omar for a private charity.
-- arguments: specialized agency of which Canada is a member; brought into relationship with the UN under article 63; international organization of which Canada is a member; purpose is peace; purpose is economic or social well-being
#ASSERT `Is my employer an international organization under regulation 1(2)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my employer an international organization under regulation 1(2)` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my employer an international organization under regulation 1(2)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

§ `Regulations 2 to 6 -- work the Regulations include in insurable employment`

-- Priya teaches in a provincial school; the province signed an agreement to waive the exclusion and insure all its employees.
-- arguments: in Canada; appointed under the public service Act; remunerated under it; employed by an agent of the province; would be insurable but for 5(2)(c); agreement entered into; waives the exclusion; insures all employees
#ASSERT `Is my work for a provincial government included in insurable employment under regulation 2` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work for a provincial government included in insurable employment under regulation 2` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)    -- the province has made no agreement
-- Quentin works in Canada for a foreign embassy that consented in writing; Rosa for one that did not, with no old consent.
-- arguments: in Canada; employer is a foreign government or subdivision; employer is an international organization; would be insurable but for 5(2)(d) and (e); consents in writing; an old consent under the Unemployment Insurance Regulations; that consent revoked
#ASSERT `Is my work for a foreign government or an international organization included in insurable employment under regulation 3` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work for a foreign government or an international organization included in insurable employment under regulation 3` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is my work for a foreign government or an international organization included in insurable employment under regulation 3` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE     -- Sunil's employer is an international organization whose pre-1996 consent still stands
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work for a foreign government or an international organization included in insurable employment under regulation 3` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE)   -- ... but if it was revoked, it does not
-- Tamsin is a deckhand on a Canadian-registered ferry on a route between Canadian and American ports.
-- arguments: on a ship outside or partly outside Canada; would be insurable in Canada; Canadian registry; regularly between ports outside Canada; chartered to a non-resident; chartered to a resident; regularly from a Canadian port; controlled in Canada; owner in Canada; agreement
#ASSERT `Is my work on a ship outside Canada included in insurable employment under regulation 4` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work on a ship outside Canada included in insurable employment under regulation 4` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)   -- Ugo's Canadian ship sails between foreign ports and is chartered to a non-resident
#ASSERT `Is my work on a ship outside Canada included in insurable employment under regulation 4` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE      -- a foreign ship chartered to a Canadian and sailing from a Canadian port
-- Vera, who lives in Calgary, works in Mexico for a Calgary firm, and Mexican law does not insure it.
-- arguments: outside Canada; on a ship described in regulation 4; ordinarily resides in Canada; employer in Canada; would be insurable in Canada; not insurable under the other country's laws
#ASSERT `Is my work outside Canada included in insurable employment under regulation 5` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work outside Canada included in insurable employment under regulation 5` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
-- Reg 6. Each question ends with "a provision of these Regulations excludes my work".
-- arguments: union member employed by the union; conducting union business; picketing; excluded
#ASSERT `Is my work for my union included in insurable employment under regulation 6(a)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work for my union included in insurable employment under regulation 6(a)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work for my union included in insurable employment under regulation 6(a)` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)    -- "unless it is excluded ... by any provision of these Regulations"
#ASSERT `Is my work as an apprentice or trainee included in insurable employment under regulation 6(b)` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work as an apprentice or trainee included in insurable employment under regulation 6(b)` FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is my work as a member of the clergy or of a religious order included in insurable employment under regulation 6(c)` TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my work as a member of the clergy or of a religious order included in insurable employment under regulation 6(c)` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work as a member of the clergy or of a religious order included in insurable employment under regulation 6(c)` TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- Wen cuts hair in a shop she does not own; Xavier owns his barbershop.
-- arguments: employed in a barbering or hairdressing establishment; provides the services; owner or operator; excluded
#ASSERT `Is my work in a barbering or hairdressing establishment included in insurable employment under regulation 6(d)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work in a barbering or hairdressing establishment included in insurable employment under regulation 6(d)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
-- Yusuf drives a taxi he owns half of (exactly 50 per cent: not more); Zoe owns 60 per cent of her taxi.
-- arguments: employed as a driver of a passenger vehicle; the portion owned; owner or operator of the business; operator of the public authority; excluded
#ASSERT `Is my work as a driver of a passenger vehicle included in insurable employment under regulation 6(e)` TRUE 0.5 FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work as a driver of a passenger vehicle included in insurable employment under regulation 6(e)` TRUE 0.6 FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Abel holds an office as defined in the Canada Pension Plan in a federal department.
-- arguments: holds an office; in the federal administration; provincial public service of an insuring province; agent body; office in a union; elected by popular vote; elected or appointed in a representative capacity; already included by paragraph (a); excluded
#ASSERT `Is my work as the holder of an office included in insurable employment under regulation 6(f)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my work as the holder of an office included in insurable employment under regulation 6(f)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE      -- Bela is an elected union officer
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work as the holder of an office included in insurable employment under regulation 6(f)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE)   -- ... already included by paragraph (a)
-- Cleo is placed by an agency to work for a client, under the client's direction, and the agency pays her.
-- arguments: placed by an agency; for and under the direction and control of a client; paid by the agency; excluded
#ASSERT `Is my work placed by an agency included in insurable employment under regulation 6(g)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work placed by an agency included in insurable employment under regulation 6(g)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE)

§ `Regulations 7 to 9 -- work the Regulations take out of insurable employment`

-- Dev is a monk who has taken a vow of poverty; his pay goes straight to the order. Ella has taken no vow.
-- arguments: member of a religious order; vow of poverty; pay paid directly to the order; pay paid by the person to the order
#ASSERT `Is my work as a member of a religious order excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(b)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work as a member of a religious order excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(b)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE)
-- Farid's premiums are paid under a United States state's law because of the 1942 Agreement (7(c)(i)); Gwen's under the Railroad Unemployment Insurance Act (7(c)(ii)); Hal's under a state law but not by reason of that Agreement.
-- arguments: premiums under a state's law; by reason of the 1942 Agreement; premiums under the Railroad Unemployment Insurance Act
#ASSERT `Is my work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(c) because premiums are payable in the United States` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(c) because premiums are payable in the United States` FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(c) because premiums are payable in the United States` TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- Ines lives in Detroit and works in Windsor; premiums are payable for those services under United States law (7(d)).
-- arguments: work in Canada; resides in another country; premiums payable under that country's laws for the services in Canada
#ASSERT `Is my work in Canada excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(d) because I live in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work in Canada excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(d) because I live in another country` TRUE TRUE FALSE)
-- Jin was hired for one flood rescue by an employer who does not regularly employ him (7(e)).
-- arguments: for a rescue operation; regularly employed by that employer
#ASSERT `Is my work on a rescue operation excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(e)` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work on a rescue operation excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(e)` TRUE TRUE)
-- Kira's work is under a 59(b) measure set up to give participants work experience (7(f)(ii)).
-- arguments: under a 59(b) measure; under a section 63 agreement measure; established to help start businesses; established to give work experience
#ASSERT `Is my work under an employment support measure excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(f)` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work under an employment support measure excluded from insurable employment under regulation 7(f)` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Leo worked six days at a travelling carnival for an employer who does not regularly employ him; Mia worked seven days.
-- arguments: in connection with a circus, fair, parade, carnival or similar activity; as an entertainer; regularly employed; days in the year
#ASSERT `Is my short job at a circus, fair or similar activity excluded from insurable employment under regulation 8(1)(a)` TRUE FALSE FALSE 6
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my short job at a circus, fair or similar activity excluded from insurable employment under regulation 8(1)(a)` TRUE FALSE FALSE 7)
-- Noor worked 20 hours on a 2025 municipal election for a municipality that does not regularly employ her; Oscar worked 35 hours.
-- arguments: employer is Canada, a province, a municipality or a school board; for a referendum or election; regularly employed; the year; hours in the year
#ASSERT `Is my short job for an election or referendum excluded from insurable employment under regulation 8(1)(c)` TRUE TRUE FALSE 2025 20
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my short job for an election or referendum excluded from insurable employment under regulation 8(1)(c)` TRUE TRUE FALSE 2025 35)
-- Pia's fair job became regular employment on 20 October 2026 (the day matters for a fair job; the hour is 0). Quinn's election job became regular on 20 October 2026 at 9.
-- arguments: excluded under 8(1)(a) or (c); became a regular employment; the day; the hour
#ASSERT `From when is my short job insurable once it becomes a regular employment under regulation 8(2)` TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 10 20) 9 EQUALS JUST (`er1 Day and hour` OF (YMD 2026 10 20), 9)
#ASSERT `From when is my short job insurable once it becomes a regular employment under regulation 8(2)` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 20) 9 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Rafa worked eight days in a year at three fairs for one employer, the first beginning on 1 June 2026 (the total exceeds six days); Sana six.
-- arguments: employed by the same employer in jobs excluded under 8(1)(a); total days; the day the total period began
#ASSERT `From which day are my short jobs for the same employer insurable together under regulation 8(3)` TRUE 8 (YMD 2026 6 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 6 1)
#ASSERT `From which day are my short jobs for the same employer insurable together under regulation 8(3)` TRUE 6 (YMD 2026 6 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Tariq worked 36 hours in a year at several election jobs for one employer, the first beginning at 8 on 1 June 2026.
-- arguments: employed by the same employer in jobs excluded under 8(1)(c); total hours; the day; the hour
#ASSERT `From which hour are my short election or referendum jobs for the same employer insurable together under regulation 8(4)` TRUE 36 (YMD 2026 6 1) 8 EQUALS JUST (`er1 Day and hour` OF (YMD 2026 6 1), 8)
#ASSERT `From which hour are my short election or referendum jobs for the same employer insurable together under regulation 8(4)` TRUE 34 (YMD 2026 6 1) 8 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Uma keeps bees on a farm; Vik does a kind of work the list does not mention, for the benefit of a farmer; Wes does the same for no farmer.
-- arguments: the operation; on a farm; selling is incidental; handling is incidental; operations of farming for the benefit of a farmer
#ASSERT `Is my farm work agriculture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 (a)(vi) raising bees and producing honey` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my farm work agriculture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 an operation that the definition does not list` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my farm work agriculture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 an operation that the definition does not list` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is my farm work agriculture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 (b)(i) offering for sale or selling any of the products of the operations referred to in subparagraphs (a)(i) to (ix)` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- arguments: the business of agriculture; for the benefit of a farmer
#ASSERT `Is my employer's business an agricultural enterprise under regulation 9(1)` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my employer's business an agricultural enterprise under regulation 9(1)` TRUE FALSE)
-- Xena grows seedlings; Yuri does landscape gardening on a farm's own grounds, incidental to the farm's agriculture.
-- arguments: the operation; landscape gardening incidental to the horticulture operations; landscape gardening incidental to agriculture; services at the place of the operations
#ASSERT `Is my work horticulture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 (a)(ii) propagating, producing, raising or harvesting seeds, seedlings, grafts or cuttings of vegetables, flowers, shrubs or ornamental grasses` FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my work horticulture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 (b) landscape gardening` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my work horticulture under regulation 9(1)` `er1 (b) landscape gardening` FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Zed picked fruit on a farm for five days and was paid cash; Abby for ten days and was paid cash; Bo for ten days in kind only.
-- arguments: would otherwise be insurable; in agriculture; in an agricultural enterprise; in horticulture; days in the year; paid in cash for any part
#ASSERT `Is my farm work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 9(2)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 5 TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my farm work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 9(2)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 10 TRUE)
#ASSERT `Is my farm work excluded from insurable employment under regulation 9(2)` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 10 FALSE

§ `Regulations 9.001 to 9.02 -- looking for work, suitable work, ending a self-employed agreement`

-- Cam networks and submits applications every week for jobs in his trade; Dee has stopped looking after the first week; Eve only prepared a resume once.
-- arguments: sustained; assessing opportunities; resume or cover letter; registering with job tools or agencies; workshops or job fairs; networking; contacting employers; applications; interviews; evaluations; directed toward suitable employment
#ASSERT `Are my efforts to find suitable work reasonable and customary efforts under regulation 9.001` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Are my efforts to find suitable work reasonable and customary efforts under regulation 9.001` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE)    -- not sustained
#ASSERT NOT (`Are my efforts to find suitable work reasonable and customary efforts under regulation 9.001` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE)     -- none of the nine kinds
#ASSERT NOT (`Are my efforts to find suitable work reasonable and customary efforts under regulation 9.001` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)     -- not directed toward suitable employment
-- Fay can reach the job and do it, its hours fit her family, and its nature breaches no belief; Gus's hours clash with his religious observance.
-- arguments: health allows commuting; health allows performing; hours clash with family obligations; hours clash with religious beliefs; nature against moral convictions; nature against religious beliefs
#ASSERT `Is this job suitable employment for me under regulation 9.002` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is this job suitable employment for me under regulation 9.002` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is this job suitable employment for me under regulation 9.002` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Hiro gave the Commission written notice ending his agreement; Ida phoned.
-- arguments: gives the Commission a notice; the notice is in writing
#ASSERT `Is my notice ending my agreement with the Commission in the form regulation 9.01 requires` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my notice ending my agreement with the Commission in the form regulation 9.01 requires` TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Is my withdrawal of that notice in the form regulation 9.02 requires` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my withdrawal of that notice in the form regulation 9.02 requires` TRUE FALSE)

§ `Regulations 9.1 to 11 -- hours of insurable employment`

-- Jo, paid by the hour, worked 120 hours at her ordinary rate and 6 at an overtime rate: 126 hours (an overtime hour is one hour).
-- arguments: hourly; ordinary hours; overtime hours; employer provides evidence; the evidence's hours; employer cannot establish; actual hours not known; parties agree; agreement reasonable; agreed hours; no agreement can be reached; Minister has established hours; the Minister's hours; total earnings; minimum wage; evidence of overtime; first day; last day; regulation 10.1 fixes the hours
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` TRUE 120 6 FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 0 0 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 126
-- Kai is on salary; the employer's records show he worked 160 hours and was paid for them.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 TRUE 160 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 0 0 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 160
-- Lou is not paid by the hour; the employer has no records and cannot say; Lou and the employer agreed, reasonably, on 100 hours.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE 100 FALSE FALSE 0 0 0 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 100
-- Mo's agreed 150 hours is not reasonable, so the Minister's 90 hours stand.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE 150 FALSE TRUE 90 0 0 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 90
-- Ned earned 420 dollars in a Monday-to-Friday week and nobody can find out his hours; the minimum wage was 14 dollars: 30 hours.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 420 14 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 30
-- Ola earned 700 dollars in the same week: 700 / 14 = 50, held to 35 (seven hours a day, 35 a week), unless there is evidence of overtime (then 50).
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 700 14 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 35
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 700 14 TRUE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 50
-- Pat's hours are not known, and she and her employer also agreed on 100: the regulations overlap (fork F8) and the narrower rule, reg 10(4), governs: 30 hours.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` FALSE 0 0 FALSE 0 TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 100 FALSE FALSE 0 420 14 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) FALSE EQUALS JUST 30
-- Quin's hours are those regulation 10.1 fixes (paid leave): regulations 9.1 and 10 give no answer here.
#ASSERT `How many hours of insurable employment am I considered to have worked in this period of employment under regulations 9.1 and 10` TRUE 120 6 FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 0 0 FALSE (YMD 2026 10 19) (YMD 2026 10 23) TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Rex's wages for 120 hours of work were never paid because of the premiums-regulation reasons; regulation 10 does not fix his hours.
-- arguments: earnings unpaid for the reasons in subsection 2(2); regulation 10 fixes the hours; hours actually worked
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked where part of my pay for a period of work was never paid, under regulation 9.2` TRUE FALSE 120 EQUALS JUST 120
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked where part of my pay for a period of work was never paid, under regulation 9.2` TRUE TRUE 120 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Sia must be on call (rate 15 against a working rate of 20), and at the premises for 8 hours in case she is needed, being paid for them: the premises rule governs.
-- arguments: required to be available; the rate paid; the rate if worked; hours available; required to be present at the premises; paid for those hours; hours present
#ASSERT `How many hours of standby or presence at the premises count as hours of insurable employment under regulation 10.01` TRUE 15 20 12 TRUE TRUE 8 EQUALS JUST 8
#ASSERT `How many hours of standby or presence at the premises count as hours of insurable employment under regulation 10.01` TRUE 20 20 12 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 12
#ASSERT `How many hours of standby or presence at the premises count as hours of insurable employment under regulation 10.01` TRUE 15 20 12 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Tom took two weeks of paid leave, 75 hours of normal work. Una's employer paid her 1000 dollars for leave she would have worked 80 hours in; her hourly rate is 20.
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked during a period of paid leave under regulation 10.1(1)` TRUE 75 EQUALS JUST 75
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked during leave paid as a lump sum under regulation 10.1(2)` TRUE 80 1000 0 20 EQUALS JUST 50
-- Vic was paid for a public holiday he did not work (7 hours normally); Wil worked 8 hours on his.
-- arguments: paid for a non-working day; works on that day; hours actually worked; hours normally worked
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked on a non-working day my employer pays me for under regulation 10.1(3)` TRUE FALSE 0 7 EQUALS JUST 7
#ASSERT `How many hours am I considered to have worked on a non-working day my employer pays me for under regulation 10.1(3)` TRUE TRUE 8 7 EQUALS JUST 8
-- Xia is a full-time worker paid for 30 hours a week where a statute limits full-time hours in her occupation to 32.
#ASSERT `How many hours a week am I considered to work if my occupation limits full-time hours under regulation 11(1)` TRUE 30 TRUE 32 EQUALS JUST 35
#ASSERT `How many hours a week am I considered to work if my occupation limits full-time hours under regulation 11(1)` TRUE 36 TRUE 32 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Yan is a full-time member of the Canadian Forces who must be on standby outside her hours; Zac is a reservist with three short periods and two of 6 to 24 hours.
#ASSERT `How many hours a week am I considered to work if I am a member of the Canadian Forces or a police force on standby under regulation 11(2)(a)` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST 35
#ASSERT `How many hours a week am I considered to work if I am a member of the reserve force under regulation 11(2)(b)` FALSE TRUE TRUE 3 2 0 EQUALS JUST 24.5
#ASSERT `How many hours a week am I considered to work if I am a member of the reserve force under regulation 11(2)(b)` FALSE TRUE TRUE 3 2 1 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Regulations 11.1 and 13 -- self-employed earnings and the average number of weeks`

-- The Commission's ratio for the year is 1.0123 and last year's adjusted amount was 6000 dollars (invented figures); a ratio of 0.9 on 6000 would cut below 6000 (counts as 1.0), on 7000 it gives 6300.
-- arguments: the amount before this year's adjustment; this year's ratio
#ASSERT `What is the adjusted amount of self-employed earnings for the year under regulation 11.1` 6000 1.0123 EQUALS 6073
#ASSERT `What is the adjusted amount of self-employed earnings for the year under regulation 11.1` 6000 0.9 EQUALS 6000
#ASSERT `What is the adjusted amount of self-employed earnings for the year under regulation 11.1` 7000 0.9 EQUALS 6300
-- Ada's maximum is 45 weeks and 20 weeks have been paid, including those used to establish the overpayment: (45 - 20) / 2 = 12.5. Bob's maximum is 26 and 10 have been paid: 8.
#ASSERT `What is my average number of weeks of regular benefits under regulation 13(1)` 45 20 EQUALS JUST 12.5
#ASSERT `What is my average number of weeks of regular benefits under regulation 13(1)` 45 46 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `What is my average number of weeks of benefits as a self-employed person under regulation 13(2)` 26 10 EQUALS JUST 8

§ `Regulation 14 -- interruption of earnings`

-- arguments (24): insured person; follows a period of employment; laid off; separated; consecutive days with no work and no earnings; normal weekly earnings; earnings for the week; ceased to work in that employment;
-- the reason (er1 Reason an insured person ceases to work); a day in the week the earnings fell; employed under a contract of employment; days of the usual remuneration period; the time falls during that period;
-- leave of 11(4); leave of 11(3); real estate on commission; provincial licence; licence lost; earnings mainly commissions; contract terminated; the day that happened; accepted less remunerative work; wage supplement; last day of work before it
-- Cara was laid off after five years and has had 14 days with no work and no earnings from the job.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 14 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21)
-- Dev stopped working to have a baby: his partner's pregnancy does not count, but Dev's wife Bea (normal weekly earnings 1000, earned 500 that week) stopped because of her pregnancy.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 1000 500 TRUE `er1 pregnancy` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21)
-- Eli's earnings fell exactly 40 per cent (1000 to 600), not "more than 40%": no interruption by that route.
#ASSERT NOT (`Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 1000 600 TRUE `er1 illness, injury or quarantine` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21))
-- Fay was laid off but had only six days without work: no interruption.
#ASSERT NOT (`Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 6 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21))
-- Gil is paid monthly under his contract (a 30-day period) and the time in question falls within it: no interruption occurs, even though he was laid off.
#ASSERT NOT (`Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 14 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) TRUE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21))
-- Hal's absence is a leave of subsection 11(4) of the Act: no interruption.
#ASSERT NOT (`Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 14 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21))
-- Ivy sells real estate on commission; her provincial licence was revoked.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21)
-- Jon took less well-paid work with his employer because his old work was a danger to his unborn child, and receives a provincial wage supplement.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred under regulation 14` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 1000 1000 FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 21) TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 10 16)
-- The days: Kim's earnings fell by half in the week of Wednesday 21 October 2026 because of illness: the interruption occurs at the beginning of that week, Sunday 18 October.
-- arguments: insured; a day in the week; normal weekly earnings; earnings for the week; ceased to work; the reason
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur because my earnings fell, under regulation 14(2)` TRUE (YMD 2026 10 21) 1000 500 TRUE `er1 illness, injury or quarantine` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 10 18)
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur because my earnings fell, under regulation 14(2)` TRUE (YMD 2026 10 21) 1000 600 TRUE `er1 illness, injury or quarantine` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Lia's real estate licence was suspended on Wednesday 21 October 2026.
-- arguments: real estate on commission; provincial licence; licence lost; ceased to work; the reason; the day it happened
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if I sell real estate on commission, under regulation 14(5)(a)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 10 21)
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if I sell real estate on commission, under regulation 14(5)(a)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 21) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Max earns mainly commissions under a contract that ended on Thursday 22 October 2026.
-- arguments: contract of employment; mainly commissions; contract terminated; ceased to work; the reason; the day it happened
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if my earnings are mainly commissions, under regulation 14(5)(b)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 22) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 10 22)
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if my earnings are mainly commissions, under regulation 14(5)(b)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE `er1 another reason` (YMD 2026 10 22) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Nia's last day before the less remunerative work was Friday 16 October 2026.
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if I accept less remunerative work, under regulation 14(7)` TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 10 16) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 10 16)
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings occur if I accept less remunerative work, under regulation 14(7)` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2026 10 16) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Oli, self-employed, normally gives 40 hours a week to the business and gave 20 in the week of Wednesday 21 October 2026, declaring the reduction because of illness; Pru gave 24 (exactly 40 per cent less).
-- arguments: a self-employed person of 152.07(1)(c); a day in the week of the declaration; the normal level in hours per week; the time given in the week; declares; ceases to work; the reason
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings as a self-employed person occur under regulation 14.01` TRUE (YMD 2026 10 21) 40 20 TRUE TRUE `er1 self-employed illness, injury or quarantine` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 10 18)
#ASSERT `On what day does an interruption of my earnings as a self-employed person occur under regulation 14.01` TRUE (YMD 2026 10 21) 40 24 TRUE TRUE `er1 self-employed illness, injury or quarantine` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Regulations 14.1 and 16 -- undeclared earnings and courses`

-- Quin's period of allocation began on 1 June 2001 and was to end on 1 December 2001; Rae's began on 1 September 2001.
-- arguments: earnings failed to be declared are allocated to a period of 15(4)(a), (b) or (c) as it read before August 12, 2001; the first day; the day it was to have ended
#ASSERT `On what day does the period to which my undeclared earnings are allocated end under regulation 14.1` TRUE (YMD 2001 6 1) (YMD 2001 12 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2001 8 12)
#ASSERT `On what day does the period to which my undeclared earnings are allocated end under regulation 14.1` TRUE (YMD 2001 9 1) (YMD 2002 1 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Sol paid for his own course (not referred by the Commission) and gets 1000 dollars of allowances, 200 of them for travel: 800 is deducted. Tess was referred to hers: nothing under 16(1).
-- arguments: a week of unemployment; attending; allowances payable for the week; referred by the Commission; total allowances; amounts for dependant care, travel and the like; entitled to work-sharing benefits; amounts using section 61 assistance
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my benefits for a week I attend a course or program under regulation 16(1) and (2)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 1000 200 FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 800
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my benefits for a week I attend a course or program under regulation 16(1) and (2)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 1000 200 FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my benefits for a week I attend a course or program under regulation 16(1) and (2)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 1000 200 TRUE 300 EQUALS JUST 500
-- Uri did not qualify for benefits for certain weeks, was paid 1500 dollars under Part II for a course in those weeks, and later became entitled to regular benefits for the same weeks.
-- arguments: does not meet the qualifying conditions; disentitled; disqualified; paid under Part II for a course; paid by a 59(b) work-experience measure; later entitled to regular benefits for the same weeks; the total paid under Part II
#ASSERT `How much of what I was paid under Part II of the Act will be deducted from my benefits for the same weeks under regulation 16(3)` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE 1500 EQUALS JUST 1500
#ASSERT `How much of what I was paid under Part II of the Act will be deducted from my benefits for the same weeks under regulation 16(3)` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE 1500 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Regulations 17 and 17.1 -- the regional rate of unemployment`

-- Vee lives in a region that section 5 of Schedule I describes; the three latest monthly rates are 6, 7 and 8 per cent (average 7 per cent) and the 12-month average, 9 per cent, is not asked for her region.
-- arguments: the section of Schedule I; Statistics Canada publishes the rate; the rates include the estimate for status Indians living on reserves; the rates of the third last, second last and last months; the 12-month average; Statistics Canada has determined an average; its three-month average; its 12-month average
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 5 TRUE TRUE 0.06 0.07 0.08 0.09 FALSE 0 0 EQUALS JUST 0.07
-- Wade lives in a region that section 13 describes: the greater of the three-month average 7 per cent and the 12-month average 9 per cent.
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 13 TRUE TRUE 0.06 0.07 0.08 0.09 FALSE 0 0 EQUALS JUST 0.09
-- Xan's region's rate is not published for confidentiality; Statistics Canada determined 7.5 per cent (three months) and 8 per cent (12 months).
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 5 FALSE TRUE 0 0 0 0 TRUE 0.075 0.08 EQUALS JUST 0.075
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 13 FALSE TRUE 0 0 0 0 TRUE 0.075 0.08 EQUALS JUST 0.08
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 5 FALSE TRUE 0 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `What is the regional rate of unemployment for my region from Statistics Canada's figures under regulation 17(1)` 5 TRUE FALSE 0.06 0.07 0.08 0.09 FALSE 0 0 EQUALS NOTHING    -- rates that do not include the estimate required by reg 17(4)
-- Yves is ordinarily resident in one region: section 7 takes that region's rate, 8 per cent. Zoe's region cannot be determined with certainty among three (7, 9 and 5 per cent): the highest, 9 per cent. Abe lives outside Canada and asks about section 12: no rate.
-- arguments: the provision; ordinarily resident outside Canada; cannot be determined with certainty; the rate of my region; near a second region; its rate; near a third region; its rate
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me, given the region I lived or last worked in, under regulation 17(1.1) to (3)` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE FALSE 0.08 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 0.08
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me, given the region I lived or last worked in, under regulation 17(1.1) to (3)` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE TRUE 0.07 TRUE 0.09 TRUE 0.05 EQUALS JUST 0.09
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me, given the region I lived or last worked in, under regulation 17(1.1) to (3)` `er1 section 12 of the Act` TRUE FALSE 0.08 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- 17.1. Bea lived in Madawaska - Charlotte (own rate 6 per cent, Restigouche - Albert 10 per cent) in the week of 7 January 2001: the special rate 9 per cent prevails over her ordinary 6.
-- arguments: the provision; outside Canada; the Sunday of the week of subsection 10(1); my region; my region's rate; the neighbour's rate; there was a first day after March 12, 2011 on which the hours and weeks were the same; that day; reg 17(1.1) to (3) gives a rate; that rate
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2001 1 7) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE 0.06 EQUALS JUST 0.09
-- Cy, in the same region, week of 2 January 2005: 7 per cent (reg 17.1(6)(a)).
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2005 1 2) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE 0.06 EQUALS JUST 0.07
-- Di, in Lower St. Lawrence and North Shore, week of 14 August 2011: 6.6 per cent (reg 17.1(12)(b)).
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2011 8 14) `er1 Lower St. Lawrence and North Shore` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE 0.06 EQUALS JUST 0.066
-- Ed, in Madawaska - Charlotte, week of 12 February 2012, after the special rates ceased on Sunday 10 April 2011 (the hours and weeks matched on Wednesday 6 April 2011): the ordinary 6 per cent applies.
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2012 2 12) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 TRUE (YMD 2011 4 6) TRUE 0.06 EQUALS JUST 0.06
-- Flo lived outside Canada and was last employed in Madawaska - Charlotte; week of 7 January 2001: 9 per cent for section 7, but nothing for section 12.
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` TRUE (YMD 2001 1 7) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 0.09
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 12 of the Act` TRUE (YMD 2001 1 7) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Gus, week of 6 January 2013, after all the periods: only reg 17(1.1) to (3) applies. Hana lives in a region reg 17.1 does not name.
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2013 1 6) `er1 Madawaska - Charlotte` 0.06 0.10 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE 0.06 EQUALS JUST 0.06
#ASSERT `Which regional rate of unemployment applies to me under regulation 17.1 if my benefit period began between September 17, 2000 and April 7, 2012` `er1 section 7 of the Act` FALSE (YMD 2005 1 2) `er1 a region not named in regulation 17.1` 0.07 0.07 FALSE (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE 0.07 EQUALS JUST 0.07
-- Reg 17.1(18): the Commission published the notice in the Gazette and on its website, as soon as possible; or it did not publish it on the website.
-- arguments: the condition of (16) or (17) is satisfied; published in the Canada Gazette; published on its website; as soon as possible
#ASSERT `Has the Commission published the notice that a special regional rate of unemployment has stopped having effect, under regulation 17.1(18)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Has the Commission published the notice that a special regional rate of unemployment has stopped having effect, under regulation 17.1(18)` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
